<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>sum-of-geometric-sequence-ii-q1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A rubber ball is dropped in the school laboratory. The heights of the first three bounces are:</p>
<p>\({@t1@}, \; {@t2@}, \; {@t3@}, \; \ldots\) cm</p>

<p>(a) Find the sum of the heights of all the bounces, in cm.</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the sum of the heights of the first {@num@} bounces, in cm.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Find the sum of the heights of all the bounces after the first {@num@} bounces, in cm.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The first term is \( a = {@a@} \). Common ratio: \( r = \frac{ {@t2@} }{ {@t1@} } = {@r@} \). This is between \(-1\) and 1, so the sum to infinity exists.</p>

<p>The heights form a geometric progression (GP), and the bounces continue forever. The sum to infinity of a GP with \(-1 &lt; r &lt; 1\) is:</p>

<p>\[ S_\infty = \frac{a}{1 - r} \]</p>
<p>The sum of the first \(n\) terms is:</p>
<p>\[ S_n = \frac{a(1 - r^n)}{1 - r} \]</p>


<p>(a) <strong>All the bounces:</strong></p>
<p>\[ S_\infty = \frac{a}{1 - r} = \frac{ {@a@} }{ 1 - {@r@} } = \frac{ {@a@} }{ {@1 - r@} } = {@ta1@} \text{ cm} \]</p>

<p>(b) <strong>First {@num@} bounces:</strong></p>
<p>\[ S_{ {@num@} } = \frac{ {@a@}\left(1 - \left({@r@}\right)^{ {@num@} }\right)}{ {@1 - r@} } = {@ta2@} \text{ cm} \]</p>

<p>(c) <strong>After the first {@num@} bounces:</strong></p>
<p>Take the sum of the first {@num@} bounces away from the sum to infinity:</p>
<p>\[ {@ta1@} - {@ta2@} = {@ta3@} \text{ cm} \]</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
